# Tracked Nginx log rotation policy for the production VPS (#906, parent #889 —
# first-user security baseline / ADR-061).
#
# Install to:
#   /etc/logrotate.d/nginx
# (replaces the distro-shipped file installed by `apt install nginx`, which ships
# `daily` / `rotate 14` with no size bound and is not tracked anywhere in this repo —
# it could be edited or lost on a package upgrade without anyone noticing).
#
# Why this stream matters
# ------------------------
# infra/nginx/*.conf sets no explicit access_log/error_log path, so both use nginx's
# stock defaults: /var/log/nginx/access.log and /var/log/nginx/error.log. Per #906/ADR-061,
# the Nginx access log is the one place a real client address reliably lands for the
# public, unauthenticated surfaces (the TikTok webhook receiver, the demo endpoints, and
# the OAuth callback/connect surface all sit in front of the app — see
# infra/nginx/api.app-juli.com.conf and infra/nginx/rate-limits.conf). It is the
# request-level companion to the structured security events in journald (#905): journald
# has "what happened and to whom", the access log has "from where and how often".
#
# Retention: 90 days, same reasoning as infra/systemd/journald.conf.d/10-retention.conf
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kept identical to the journald window on purpose — a security investigation needs both
# streams to cover the same period, or the shorter one silently caps what can actually be
# reconstructed. See that file's header for the full "why 90 days" reasoning (single-
# founder operation, no dedicated on-call, no observability platform yet, PCI DSS's own
# 90-day floor for security-relevant logs). The distro default of `rotate 14` (14 days)
# is far short of a realistic "we noticed something looked odd, let's go check" lag for a
# deliberately slow-paced attack.
#
# Absolute bound, not merely time-based
# --------------------------------------
# `daily` + `rotate 90` alone is still only a time bound: a single day's log has no upper
# size, so a flood (or an attack, which is exactly when this matters) could grow one
# rotation's file without limit until the next scheduled rotation. `maxsize 50M` forces an
# out-of-cycle rotation the moment any one log file crosses 50MB, independent of the daily
# schedule, giving a real ceiling on any single generation.
#
# Worst-case disk math (stated, not measured — see the disk assumption in
# infra/systemd/journald.conf.d/10-retention.conf): current file + `.1` (delaycompress
# keeps the most recent rotation uncompressed for immediate grep) can each reach 50MB
# uncompressed, and up to 88 further compressed generations. Text access logs commonly
# compress 85-95%+, but assuming a pessimistic 10% compression ratio (45MB retained per
# generation) still bounds the two streams (access.log + error.log) at roughly
# 2 * (50MB + 50MB + 88 * 45MB) ~= 8.1GB worst case — comfortably inside the disk
# assumption stated in the journald drop-in. Realistic usage (normal traffic, logs that
# actually compress well) is expected to be well under 1GB combined.
/var/log/nginx/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 90
    maxsize 50M
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    create 0640 www-data adm
    sharedscripts
    postrotate
        [ -f /run/nginx.pid ] && kill -USR1 "$(cat /run/nginx.pid)"
    endscript
}
